The Security Council,

Reaffirming the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations,

Recalling all its relevant resolutions on the situation in the Middle East, including the Palestinian question, in particular resolutions 2712 (2023), 2720 (2023), 2728 (2024), and 2735 (2024),

Expressing grave concern over the breakdown of the cessation of hostilities, the resumption of intense military operations, and the catastrophic and deteriorating humanitarian situation facing civilians across the Gaza Strip,

Condemning all acts of terrorism, including the heinous attacks led by Hamas on 7 October 2023, the taking and continued holding of hostages, and attacks directed against civilians,

Reiterating that all parties to the conflict must comply with their obligations under international law, including international humanitarian law and international human rights law, in particular regarding the protection of civilians and civilian infrastructure,

Underscoring that a durable end to the war and a sustainable cessation of hostilities must be inextricably linked to the release of all hostages held in Gaza,

Commending the continuous and intensive diplomatic efforts of Egypt, Qatar, and the United States aimed at securing the release of hostages, ending the hostilities, and ensuring full humanitarian access,

1. Demands the immediate, unconditional, and dignified release of all remaining hostages held by Hamas and other armed groups, as well as the immediate return of the remains of deceased hostages;

2. Calls for an immediate, full, and durable ceasefire in Gaza, inextricably linked to the immediate release of hostages, in full accordance with the framework established in resolution 2735 (2024);

3. Urges both parties to immediately re-engage in good faith and without preconditions in the ongoing diplomatic negotiations facilitated by Egypt, Qatar, and the United States to reinstate the phased ceasefire agreement, secure the exchange of all remaining hostages and Palestinian prisoners, and achieve a permanent end to hostilities;

4. Demands the immediate, safe, unhindered, and sustained delivery of humanitarian assistance at scale to civilians throughout the entire Gaza Strip, through all available border crossings and entry points, and stresses the imperative of ensuring the safety and security of all humanitarian and medical personnel, facilities, and assets;

5. Rejects any attempt at demographic or territorial change in the Gaza Strip, including any actions that permanently reduce the territory of Gaza, the creation of unauthorized buffer zones, or the forced displacement of the civilian population, which is strictly prohibited under international humanitarian law;

6. Reaffirms the necessity of unifying the Gaza Strip and the West Bank under the Palestinian Authority, and reiterates its unwavering commitment to the vision of a negotiated two-State solution where two democratic States, Israel and Palestine, live side by side in peace within secure and recognized borders;

7. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

